The fix is in. `server/services/auto-apply-pipeline.ts:41-66`:

```ts
export type AutoApplySeverity = 'low' | 'medium' | 'high' | 'critical';

const DEFAULT_MAX_SEVERITY: AutoApplySeverity = 'medium';

function isAutoApplySeverity(value: unknown): value is AutoApplySeverity {
  return value === 'low' || value === 'medium' || value === 'high' || value === 'critical';
}

function toAutoApplySeverity(value: unknown): AutoApplySeverity {
  if (isAutoApplySeverity(value)) return value;
  if (value != null && value !== '') {
    generalLog.warn(`[AutoApply] Unrecognised maxSeverity "${String(value)}" — falling back to ${DEFAULT_MAX_SEVERITY}`);
  }
  return DEFAULT_MAX_SEVERITY;
}
```

and line 188 is now `maxSeverity: toAutoApplySeverity(settings.autoApplyMaxSeverity)`.

**Why this shape.** `governanceSettings.autoApplyMaxSeverity` is `varchar(50).default("medium").notNull()` (`drizzle/schema.ts:4703`), so Drizzle infers plain `string` — nothing constrains the column to the four severities. The old `(x as unknown) ?? 'medium'` collapsed to `{}` because `unknown` isn't nullable-narrowable, so the `??` left branch stayed `unknown`/`{}`. A cast to the union would have been just as wrong in the other direction: it would assert a guarantee the DB doesn't make, and a stray value would then flow into `SEVERITY_ORDER[config.maxSeverity]` and silently yield `undefined`. The type guard makes the narrowing real — an unexpected column value now lands on the documented default and logs it.

I also pointed `AutoApplyConfig.maxSeverity` and `setAutoApplyMode`'s parameter at the named alias; both are structurally identical to the inline unions they replace, and I checked the callers (`telemetry-router.ts:243,553`, `autonomy-gap-v2.test.ts:237`) — all pass literals in the union, so nothing external shifts.

**One thing I could not do: run the verification.** Every attempt to execute the typecheck was refused by the permission layer — `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`, `./node_modules/.bin/tsc`, `node ./node_modules/typescript/bin/tsc`, even `node -e "console.log(1)"` and the serena diagnostics tool. Node execution appears to be entirely unapproved in this session; plain `grep`/`sed` work fine. So the claim that this compiles clean is reasoned from the schema types and caller inspection, not observed from the compiler. Please approve one of those commands (or run it yourself) to confirm zero errors before treating the task as verified.

